  "text": "Solution 1 gives the correct answer, $c = 18/7$. I could not verify this by running anything, so it rests on my recollection of the literature rather than a full check.\n\n- **Solution 1 ($18/7$):** This matches my recollection of a published result on proper conflict-free $(\\text{degree}+2)$-choosability: connected graphs $G \\neq C_5$ with $\\operatorname{mad}(G) < 18/7$ are proper conflict-free $(\\text{degree}+2)$-choosable, and the bound is sharp. Its discharging arithmetic is internally consistent (a 2-vertex gets $2 + 2\\cdot\\tfrac{2}{7} = \\tfrac{18}{7}$, and the other vertex types stay at or above that). The reducibility arguments are sketchy, but the final value is what the question asks for.\n- **Solution 2 ($36/11$):** The threshold is not supported. Its discharging step is hand-waved (\"a refined analysis… yields…\"), and its claimed tightness example is not checked.\n- **Solution 3 ($18/5$):** The charge scheme assumes 2-vertices can only neighbour vertices of degree $4^+$, which its reducibility claims do not establish. The threshold $18/5$ is also larger than $18/7$, so it would be a weaker statement than the one the literature gives.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
